From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?John=20T=C3=B6rnblom?= <john.tornblom@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:36:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/28] ac, aco, radv: typed vertex fetches aligned where the
 kernel wants them

On GFX7-9 RADV and ACO fetch a vertex attribute with one typed buffer
load whatever its address's alignment, because the amdgpu kernel sets
SH_MEM_CONFIG.ALIGNMENT_MODE to UNALIGNED. The PS4's kernel does not,
and there a typed load whose address is not a multiple of its element's
size reads the wrong bytes, without a fault: two ports found it on the
console independently. The DolphinPS4/Crema RADV port saw every skinned
model in Sonic Lost World break, an 8_8 fetch at an odd address (an
R8G8B8A8 attribute whose first channel is unused); the orbis-ports
mesa-ps4 port measured 98 failures in dEQP-VK.pipeline.monolithic.
vertex_input, 97 of which pass with the fetches split, as on GFX6.

ac_compiler_info.has_unaligned_typed_vtx_fetch says whether the
unaligned fetch works: set for GFX7-9 as before, and cleared by the PS4
winsys. ac_get_safe_fetch_size takes it in place of the GFX level, ACO
and LLVM pass it from their compiler info, and RADV's dynamic vertex
input marks misaligned attributes for the prolog by it.

The cost is extra VMEM instructions for sub-32-bit attributes whose
alignment is not known; 32-bit formats use untyped loads and are not
affected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Bb93U7uY5qVNuEcHDggEfE
